<v Maya>Which is huge.</v>

15
00:01:33.070 --> 00:01:40.817
<v Daniel>It is. But the critical detail, the really important part, was the impact. It was only saving them about 1.4% of their work hours.</v>

16
00:01:42.826 --> 00:01:51.532
<v Maya>Yeah, that 1.4% is the crucial number here. I mean, it was a staggering rate of adoption for a brand new technology, sure. But saving a few minutes a day is,</v>

17
00:01:52.533 --> 00:01:52.753
well,

18
00:01:53.394 --> 00:01:56.256
<v Maya>it's hardly the robot butler revolution we were promised.</v>

19
00:01:56.356 --> 00:01:57.076
<v Daniel>No, not at all.</v>

20
00:01:57.516 --> 00:02:05.262
<v Maya>It was a foothold, which really begs the question, right? If it's only saving a tiny bit of time, what exactly are people doing with it?</v>

21
00:02:05.562 --> 00:02:08.044
<v Daniel>And that is exactly what we're getting into today. Yes.</v>

22
00:02:08.557 --> 00:02:23.309
<v Maya>To answer that, we have a brand new working paper. This is from August 2026 by Bick, Blandon, Deming and Tyler Schumacher. And it's called What Work Does Generative AI Do? So, OK, let's unpack this. Let's do it. We all know AI can like</v>

23
00:02:23.930 --> 00:02:34.479
<v Maya>write a sonnet or debug a Python script. But how do you actually measure its impact across thousands of different jobs in the real world without just, you know, taking the software companies at their word?</v>

24
00:02:35.019 --> 00:02:43.382
<v Daniel>It's a massive measurement challenge, truly. To solve it, these economists apply a very specific framework. They actually don't look at jobs at all.</v>

25
00:02:43.702 --> 00:02:44.322
<v Maya>Wait, really?</v>

26
00:02:44.342 --> 00:02:51.085
<v Daniel>Yeah, they look at tasks. The underlying economic theory here is that a job is just a bundle of distinct tasks.</v>

27
00:02:51.785 --> 00:02:52.545
<v Maya>Okay, makes sense.</v>

28
00:02:52.825 --> 00:02:59.788
<v Daniel>So think about a nurse. That job involves checking vitals, updating charts, comforting patients, coordinating with doctors. It's a bundle.</v>

29
00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:00.408
<v Maya>Right.</v>

30
00:03:01.022 --> 00:03:10.709
<v Daniel>So when a technology arrives that can perform a specific task, say, synthesizing patient notes for a medical chart, well, that specific task loses value in the labor market.</v>

31
00:03:11.090 --> 00:03:12.931
<v Maya>Because it's now cheap and easy to execute.</v>

32
00:03:13.151 --> 00:03:18.495
<v Daniel>Exactly. The machine handles the routine data entry. So no one is going to pay a premium for a human to do it.</v>

33
00:03:19.356 --> 00:03:20.457
<v Maya>But the nurse isn't fired.</v>

34
00:03:20.778 --> 00:03:31.162
<v Daniel>No, because the economic framework tells us something else happens simultaneously. The complementary tasks, you know, the things the technology absolutely cannot do, they actually gain value.</v>

35
00:03:31.682 --> 00:03:32.123
<v Maya>Oh, I see.</v>

36
00:03:32.143 --> 00:03:44.227
<v Daniel>Yeah, because the nurse isn't spending an hour writing notes anymore. The act of comforting the patient or making a complex empathetic judgment call in real time, that becomes the core value of that role.</v>

37
00:03:44.787 --> 00:03:45.688
<v Maya>The human element.</v>

38
00:03:45.888 --> 00:03:55.528
<v Daniel>Right. So to truly understand AI's impact, you cannot survey jobs. You have to measure adoption at this very granular task-by-task level.</v>

39
00:03:56.208 --> 00:04:07.316
<v Maya>Okay, but practically speaking, how do you even measure that? I mean, you can't just hand a 300-page survey to a worker and ask them to check off which of the tens of thousands of potential tasks they did that week.</v>

40
00:04:07.576 --> 00:04:13.700
<v Daniel>You're totally right. Historically, you couldn't. It's actually known in economics as the impossible survey problem.</v>

41
00:04:14.400 --> 00:04:14.880
<v Maya>Fitting name.</v>

42
00:04:15.181 --> 00:04:23.485
<v Daniel>Yeah. But to get around it, these researchers built a brilliantly clever data engine. They used the Real-Time Population Survey, or RPS.</v>

43
00:04:23.605 --> 00:04:23.865
<v Maya>Okay.</v>

44
00:04:24.185 --> 00:04:35.191
<v Daniel>And over four distinct waves, so we're talking August 2025, November 2025, February 2026, and May 2026, they polled about 5,000 respondents per wave.</v>

45
00:04:35.591 --> 00:04:37.312
<v Maya>That's a solid sample size.</v>

46
00:04:37.572 --> 00:04:49.837
<v Daniel>It is. And the system starts by capturing a worker's occupation simply by letting them type it out in free text. Then, an autocoder matches that raw text to a specific official occupation.</v>

47
00:04:50.197 --> 00:04:56.842
<v Maya>Okay, so if I type in, like, middle school math teacher, the autocoder knows exactly which official government bucket that belongs in.</v>

48
00:04:56.942 --> 00:05:01.806
<v Daniel>Precisely. Got it. And once it knows the bucket, it consults a database called ONET.</v>

49
00:05:02.707 --> 00:05:03.768
<v Maya>ONET. What is that?</v>

50
00:05:04.435 --> 00:05:09.679
<v Daniel>O-Net is essentially the U.S. government's master directory of what every job actually entails. Oh,</v>

51
00:05:09.699 --> 00:05:09.999
<v Maya>wow.</v>

52
00:05:10.439 --> 00:05:22.148
<v Daniel>Yeah. It's incredibly detailed. So the survey system grabs just the top 10 most important detailed work activities for that specific occupation. And it shows only those 10 to the worker.</v>

53
00:05:22.508 --> 00:05:23.909
<v Maya>Okay. So narrowing it down.</v>

54
00:05:24.109 --> 00:05:30.474
<v Daniel>Right. And then it just asks, which of these tasks do you regularly perform? And which do you use generative AI to help with?</v>

55
00:05:30.905 --> 00:05:37.048
<v Maya>Okay, I have to stop you there for a second. Wait, this data was gathered via an online Qualtrics panel, right?</v>

56
00:05:37.248 --> 00:05:37.648
<v Daniel>That's right.</v>

57
00:05:38.328 --> 00:05:49.974
<v Maya>That's not exactly a gold standard random door-to-door sample of the public. Plus, I mean, it relies entirely on self-reported use. Can we actually trust people to accurately report their AI habits?</v>

58
00:05:50.154 --> 00:05:51.094
<v Daniel>That's a fair question.</v>

59
00:05:51.435 --> 00:05:56.357
<v Maya>Because, you know, half the people using it to write their daily update emails probably want to hide that fact from their boss.</v>

60
00:05:56.853 --> 00:06:11.582
<v Daniel>No, it is a highly valid objection to the methodology. Self-reporting always carries a risk of bias. Workers might under-report if they fear the technology might replace them. Or on the flip side, they might not even realize a software feature they use daily is technically</v>

61
00:06:11.642 --> 00:06:12.482
<v Daniel>generative AI.</v>

62
00:06:12.782 --> 00:06:14.844
<v Maya>Right, like an autocomplete feature or something.</v>

63
00:06:14.884 --> 00:06:22.807
<v Daniel>Exactly. However, the researchers defend the data's validity because they meticulously weight the sample to mirror the government's current population</v>

64
00:06:22.848 --> 00:06:23.888
<v Maya>survey.</v>

65
00:06:24.268 --> 00:06:29.890
<v Daniel>By matching the demographic and employment status breakdowns of the CPS, they correct for the skews of an online panel.</v>

66
00:06:30.190 --> 00:06:30.871
<v Maya>That helps a lot.</v>

67
00:06:31.131 --> 00:06:39.734
<v Daniel>It does. And furthermore, the task adoption rankings held incredibly steady across all four waves from August 2025 to May 2026. And if</v>

