135
00:13:04.987 --> 00:13:05.587
<v Maya>just use it.</v>

136
00:13:05.967 --> 00:13:10.989
<v Daniel>And this raises an incredibly important question about how economists have been trying to measure this whole phenomenon.</v>

137
00:13:11.549 --> 00:13:12.549
<v Maya>Okay, where are we going with this?</v>

138
00:13:13.210 --> 00:13:26.286
<v Daniel>Well, if predicting AI use by looking at the tasks is flawed because it ignores human compliance barriers, what happens when we try to bypass the surveys entirely and just look directly at what people are typing into the chatbots?</v>

139
00:13:27.003 --> 00:13:28.804
<v Maya>We actually have a lot of data on that, too. We</v>

140
00:13:29.085 --> 00:13:29.205
<v Daniel>do.</v>

141
00:13:29.545 --> 00:13:44.036
<v Maya>The researchers compared their survey against three major background studies based on chat logs. You have a study by Honda and colleagues who mapped millions of conversations on Anthropix Cloud to O-Net tasks. Right. Chatterjee's team analyzed OpenAI's ChatGPT.</v>

142
00:13:44.696 --> 00:13:58.347
<v Maya>And Tomlinson's team analyzed 200,000 conversations on Microsoft Copilot, finding that information work heavily dominated the prompts. Yep. I mean, it sounds like a researcher's dream. You just parse the chat logs and you instantly know what the entire economy is working on.</v>

143
00:13:58.927 --> 00:14:10.978
<v Daniel>You would certainly assume that to be true. But we have to name the conflict plainly here. What people actually type into a chat bot versus what workers tell the government survey they use it for, they barely match at all.</v>

144
00:14:11.418 --> 00:14:11.718
<v Maya>Really?</v>

145
00:14:12.219 --> 00:14:18.244
<v Daniel>The correlation between these chat log measures and the survey is below 0.4. They are completely out of sync.</v>

146
00:14:18.603 --> 00:14:27.791
<v Maya>Okay, here's where it gets really interesting. Why is there such a massive disconnect between these massive data sets of chat logs and the detailed survey?</v>

147
00:14:28.911 --> 00:14:31.213
<v Daniel>It falls into what we call the generic task trap.</v>

148
00:14:32.054 --> 00:14:33.876
<v Maya>The generic task trap.</v>

149
00:14:34.036 --> 00:14:45.445
<v Daniel>Yeah. So chat classifiers, the algorithms designed by researchers to categorize millions of text prompts, they do not know the user's occupation. They just see the raw text of the prompt.</v>

150
00:14:46.386 --> 00:14:47.607
<v Maya>OK. They just see the text.</v>

151
00:14:47.868 --> 00:15:01.643
<v Daniel>Right. As a result, they end up dumping highly nuanced chats into very broad, generic buckets. For example, over 15 percent of all open AI chats are labeled by classifiers with the task, edit written materials or documents.</v>

152
00:15:02.123 --> 00:15:08.490
<v Maya>Well, that makes perfect sense for a classifier. I mean, it sees a prompt asking to fix grammar and it tags it as editing.</v>

153
00:15:08.947 --> 00:15:23.653
