162
00:16:15.037 --> 00:16:22.840
<v Maya>Okay, so those recent labor papers that use chat logs as a proxy to measure AI exposure across the economy, they are likely highly inaccurate.</v>

163
00:16:22.980 --> 00:16:23.561
<v Daniel>Highly likely.</v>

164
00:16:23.841 --> 00:16:26.002
<v Maya>They are just seeing the water usage, not the menu.</v>

165
00:16:26.082 --> 00:16:38.167
<v Daniel>Yep. So if predicting AI use by the task is flawed because of compliance barriers, and predicting it by chat logs is inaccurate because it strips away occupational context, where does the real variation actually lie?</v>

166
00:16:39.147 --> 00:16:40.448
<v Maya>It has to be the individual worker.</v>

167
00:16:41.028 --> 00:16:55.692
<v Daniel>Precisely. The paper finds massive variation among people doing the exact same work. You can have two accountants sitting at adjacent desks performing the exact same tasks under the exact same corporate compliance rules. One is using</v>

168
00:16:55.752 --> 00:16:57.832
<v Daniel>AI constantly and the other isn't touching it.</v>

169
00:16:58.332 --> 00:17:05.334
<v Maya>We have to dig into the why here. What separates those two workers? I mean, is it just age or some innate tech savviness?</v>

170
00:17:06.034 --> 00:17:06.114
It

171
00:17:06.314 --> 00:17:09.795
<v Daniel>fundamentally comes down to what economists call a fixed learning cost.</v>

172
00:17:10.448 --> 00:17:11.429
<v Maya>A fixed learning cost.</v>

173
00:17:11.629 --> 00:17:26.376
<v Daniel>Yeah. Adopting AI isn't just about being handed a software license. It requires a painful, frustrating burst of effort. A worker has to stare at a blank prompt box, figure out how to speak to the machine, learn how to verify its output,</v>

174
00:17:26.797 --> 00:17:29.558
<v Daniel>and discover the hard way where the model hallucinates or fails.</v>

175
00:17:30.198 --> 00:17:30.899
<v Maya>Which takes time.</v>

176
00:17:31.499 --> 00:17:36.241
<v Daniel>It takes hours of unpaid cognitive labor. And many workers just abandon it.</v>

177
00:17:36.922 --> 00:17:38.783
<v Maya>But for the ones who push through that frustration...</v>

178
00:17:39.382 --> 00:17:54.134
<v Daniel>For the ones who pay that fixed learning cost in one specific domain, say they spend a week figuring out how to get the AI to flawlessly draft difficult client emails, that experience creates spillovers. Suddenly, the mental barrier is gone. They are drastically more</v>

179
00:17:54.174 --> 00:17:59.458
<v Daniel>likely to start applying it to other tasks, like analyzing a spreadsheet or brainstorming a project outline.</v>

180
00:17:59.798 --> 00:18:03.982
<v Maya>So what does this all mean for the timeline of AI adoption across the economy?</v>

181
00:18:04.360 --> 00:18:16.226
<v Daniel>Well, because of these spillovers, we should forecast that adoption will inevitably deepen over time. As more individual workers finally get over that initial learning hump, they will naturally spread the tool to the other components of the job.</v>

182
00:18:16.507 --> 00:18:18.087
<v Maya>The shallow puddle will get deeper.</v>

183
00:18:18.448 --> 00:18:31.855
<v Daniel>Exactly. But I must raise the final and perhaps most vital objection to keep in mind when reading these numbers. Adoption does not equal productivity. And it certainly does not equal displacement.</v>

184
00:18:32.333 --> 00:18:47.079
<v Maya>That is a crucial distinction. Just because a worker uses AI to write a report doesn't automatically mean they are suddenly twice as fast or producing superior work. Right. The AI's output might be so generic or so flawed that it requires an hour of</v>

185
00:18:47.179 --> 00:18:54.722
<v Maya>editing, making the time savings completely negligible. Or, you know, they might just use the 20 minutes they save to take a longer lunch break.</v>

186
00:18:54.802 --> 00:18:55.602
<v Daniel>Which happens a lot.</v>

187
00:18:55.835 --> 00:19:00.180
<v Maya>Right. Using a tool does not mean the worker's job is about to be eliminated by management.</v>

188
00:19:00.500 --> 00:19:00.660
<v Daniel>No.</v>

189
00:19:01.421 --> 00:19:16.096
<v Maya>And if we connect this to the bigger picture, knowing that the individual worker's ability and willingness to pay that fixed learning cost is the true bottleneck, it profoundly shifts the imperative for our institutions, especially higher education. This is a massive revelation for</v>

190
00:19:16.156 --> 00:19:28.487
<v Maya>universities. Think about large urban public systems like CUNY in New York City. You are looking at a student body heavily comprised of early career workers, adult learners and first generation students.</v>

191
00:19:28.747 --> 00:19:28.948
<v Daniel>Yes.</v>

192
00:19:29.488 --> 00:19:35.453
<v Maya>These are the exact populations that frequently step into the clerical and administrative roles we discussed earlier.</v>

193
00:19:35.834 --> 00:19:43.901
<v Daniel>The exact roles that are heavily exposed to AI on paper, but where workers are paralyzed by strict compliance rules and a lack of specific technical training.</v>

194
00:19:44.469 --> 00:19:56.636
<v Maya>So if who adopts the technology matters just as much as which tasks the technology can perform, then simply teaching students how to use AI is in itself a massive labor market intervention.</v>

195
00:19:57.056 --> 00:19:57.556
<v Daniel>Absolutely.</v>

196
00:19:58.017 --> 00:20:04.520
<v Maya>Universities can't just offer a generic intro to prompts class. They have to bridge the gap by teaching task level AI skills.</v>

197
00:20:05.021 --> 00:20:15.838
<v Daniel>They need to teach a student how to use an isolated AI environment to securely process an insurance claim. Or, you know, how to execute legal discovery using a language model without violating client privacy.</v>

198
00:20:16.779 --> 00:20:19.500
<v Maya>And it opens up an entirely new avenue for credentialing too.</v>

199
00:20:19.520 --> 00:20:20.660
<v Daniel>Oh, completely.</v>

200
00:20:21.401 --> 00:20:36.387
<v Maya>Employers have legions of administrative staff who are completely terrified to adopt the technology because of top-down fear. So universities have the opportunity to partner with those employers, provide a safe sandbox environment for the worker to pay that fixed learning cost, and then</v>

201
00:20:36.487 --> 00:20:37.608
<v Maya>send them back with a credential.</v>

202
00:20:37.980 --> 00:20:42.744
<v Daniel>A credential that proves they know how to navigate AI safely in a highly regulated environment.</v>

203
00:20:43.104 --> 00:20:52.012
<v Maya>Yes. If higher education doesn't step in to provide that bridge, we just leave these early career and first-gen students stranded on the wrong side of the AI divide.</v>

204
00:20:52.513 --> 00:21:04.503
<v Daniel>The frontier of artificial intelligence is no longer just about engineers building larger, more capable models. The actual economic frontier is training individual workers to navigate the models that already exist.</v>

205
00:21:04.997 --> 00:21:15.902
<v Maya>I love that. To quickly recap our journey through the data today for you, the listener, AI adoption right now is a shallow puddle. It reaches across a huge parking lot of occupations, but it isn't deep.</v>

206
00:21:16.122 --> 00:21:16.302
<v Daniel>Nope.</v>

207
00:21:17.082 --> 00:21:30.188
<v Maya>The theoretical exposure scores that predicted mass disruption, they missed the mark because they ignored human barriers like compliance and lack of training. The millions of chat logs are misleading because they strip away a worker's actual occupational context.</v>

208
00:21:30.628 --> 00:21:32.249
<v Daniel>The water meter, not the menu.</v>

209
00:21:32.712 --> 00:21:40.660
<v Maya>Exactly. The real bottleneck holding back the future of work is the individual worker needing the time and permission to pay that initial learning cost.</v>

210
00:21:41.241 --> 00:21:44.744
<v Daniel>And as we consider those barriers, I want to leave you with a final thought to mull over.</v>

211
00:21:44.784 --> 00:21:45.224
<v Maya>Please do.</v>

212
00:21:45.980 --> 00:21:59.947
<v Daniel>We saw clearly in the data that high AI adoption relies heavily on a worker having personal autonomy and the permission to experiment. High autonomy means high adoption. Right. So are highly regulated, top-down companies</v>

213
00:22:00.527 --> 00:22:02.969
<v Daniel>inadvertently designing themselves into obsolescence?</v>

214
00:22:03.449 --> 00:22:03.929
<v Maya>Oh, wow.</v>

215
00:22:04.229 --> 00:22:11.233
<v Daniel>By forbidding the very experimentation their workers desperately need to figure out these tools? Are they ensuring their own failure in the labor market?</v>

216
00:22:11.653 --> 00:22:26.312
<v Maya>It is the ultimate corporate irony. In trying to mitigate the risk of the technology, they completely prevent their own workforce from learning how to survive it. Thank you for joining us on this deep dive. Remember, being well-informed isn't just about knowing the data.</v>

217
00:22:26.852 --> 00:22:38.988
<v Maya>It's about knowing what to do with it. The future isn't a silver jumpsuit and a robot butler doing your laundry. It's a spreadsheet. It's an administrative workflow. And it's up to us to learn how to navigate it.</v>
